Q: Angular: Element implicitly has an &#39;any&#39; type because expression of type &#39;string&#39; can&#39;t be used to index type A: <p>The issue is that declaring <code>MESSAGES</code> like this gives it an implicit type based on <code>EN</code> and <code>IT</code> and therefore expects a specific key from those objects when it is accessed.</p>
<p>You can prevent the error by explicitly typing <code>MESSAGES</code> like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const MESSAGES: {[langKey in Lang]: Record&lt;string, string&gt;} = {
    en: EN,
    it: IT
};
</code></pre>
<p>This gives you the benefit of still being able to pass a <code>string</code> to <code>get</code> that has no translation key.</p>
